<main id="main">
<section class="p-hero"><div class="p-hero-img"><img src="/assets/img/photos/paper-writing.webp" srcset="/assets/img/photos/paper-writing-640.webp 640w, /assets/img/photos/paper-writing.webp 960w" sizes="100vw" width="960" height="640" alt="" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"></div><div class="wrap p-hero-in"><nav class="crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><a href="/">Home</a><span class="sep" aria-hidden="true">/</span><span aria-current="page">Claim terms defined</span></nav><p class="kicker">Reference</p><h1>Insurance Claim Terms Defined in Plain English</h1><p class="lede">41 terms you’ll meet in California policies, estimates and appraisal awards. Type to filter.</p></div></section>
<div class="wrap two-col"><article class="prose"><div class="filter"><label for="gloss-filter">Search the glossary</label><input id="gloss-filter" type="search" placeholder="Try “holdback” or “FAIR Plan”" autocomplete="off"><p class="hint" data-gloss-count></p></div><div class="az"><a href="#g-A">A</a><a href="#g-B">B</a><a href="#g-C">C</a><a href="#g-D">D</a><a href="#g-E">E</a><a href="#g-F">F</a><a href="#g-H">H</a><a href="#g-I">I</a><a href="#g-L">L</a><a href="#g-M">M</a><a href="#g-O">O</a><a href="#g-P">P</a><a href="#g-R">R</a><a href="#g-S">S</a><a href="#g-U">U</a><a href="#g-X">X</a></div><dl class="glossary"><div class="term" id="g-A"><dt>Actual cash value (ACV)</dt><dd>Replacement cost minus depreciation. Many policies pay ACV first and the rest after repairs.</dd></div><div class="term"><dt>Additional living expenses (ALE)</dt><dd>Extra costs of living elsewhere while your home is repaired, such as rent and meals above normal.</dd></div><div class="term"><dt>Appraisal</dt><dd>A policy process for resolving disputes over the amount of a covered loss using two appraisers and an umpire.</dd></div><div class="term"><dt>Appraisal award</dt><dd>The written, itemized decision signed by any two panel members that sets the amount of loss.</dd></div><div class="term"><dt>Appraisal demand</dt><dd>A written request by either party to resolve an amount-of-loss dispute through appraisal.</dd></div><div class="term" id="g-B"><dt>Building code upgrade coverage</dt><dd>Coverage for repair costs required by current building codes, often limited to a percentage of the dwelling limit.</dd></div><div class="term" id="g-C"><dt>California Earthquake Authority (CEA)</dt><dd>A publicly managed, privately funded provider of residential earthquake insurance sold through participating insurers.</dd></div><div class="term"><dt>California FAIR Plan</dt><dd>An insurer-funded association that offers basic fire coverage when private insurance isn’t available.</dd></div><div class="term"><dt>Causation</dt><dd>What caused the damage. Generally treated as a coverage question rather than a valuation question.</dd></div><div class="term"><dt>CDI</dt><dd>The California Department of Insurance, the state agency that regulates insurers, led by the elected Insurance Commissioner.</dd></div><div class="term"><dt>Coverage A</dt><dd>The dwelling limit on a homeowner policy.</dd></div><div class="term"><dt>Coverage C</dt><dd>The personal property, or contents, limit on a homeowner policy.</dd></div><div class="term" id="g-D"><dt>Debris removal</dt><dd>Coverage for removing damaged property, sometimes an added percentage of the dwelling limit.</dd></div><div class="term"><dt>Declarations page</dt><dd>The summary page listing limits, deductibles, endorsements and the policy period.</dd></div><div class="term"><dt>Deductible</dt><dd>The portion of a covered loss you pay before the policy pays.</dd></div><div class="term"><dt>Depreciation</dt><dd>A reduction in value for age, wear and condition, subtracted from replacement cost to find ACV.</dd></div><div class="term"><dt>Difference in conditions (DIC)</dt><dd>A policy that covers perils the FAIR Plan excludes, such as water damage and theft.</dd></div><div class="term"><dt>Disinterested appraiser</dt><dd>An appraiser with no financial stake in the outcome and no disqualifying relationship with the parties.</dd></div><div class="term"><dt>Dwelling fire policy</dt><dd>A basic policy often used for rentals, covering named perils and frequently paying ACV.</dd></div><div class="term" id="g-E"><dt>Extended replacement cost</dt><dd>Added coverage above the dwelling limit, often 20% to 50%, when rebuilding costs more than the limit.</dd></div><div class="term" id="g-F"><dt>Fair Claims Settlement Practices Regulations</dt><dd>California regulations setting standards and time frames for how insurers handle claims.</dd></div><div class="term"><dt>Fire following earthquake</dt><dd>Fire caused by an earthquake, generally covered under the standard fire policy even though earthquake damage is excluded.</dd></div><div class="term" id="g-H"><dt>HO-3</dt><dd>The most common homeowner policy form, covering the dwelling on an open-perils basis.</dd></div><div class="term"><dt>HO-6</dt><dd>A condominium unit-owner policy covering interiors, improvements and contents.</dd></div><div class="term"><dt>Holdback</dt><dd>The recoverable depreciation an insurer pays after repairs or replacement are complete.</dd></div><div class="term" id="g-I"><dt>Insurance Code §2071</dt><dd>The California statute containing the standard form fire policy, including its appraisal provision.</dd></div><div class="term" id="g-L"><dt>Like kind and quality</dt><dd>Replacement with materials or items of similar type, grade and features.</dd></div><div class="term"><dt>Line item</dt><dd>A single entry in an estimate, such as a quantity of drywall or roofing, with its unit price.</dd></div><div class="term" id="g-M"><dt>Master policy</dt><dd>The insurance policy an HOA carries on common areas and, often, the building structure.</dd></div><div class="term"><dt>Matching</dt><dd>Whether repaired areas can reasonably match undamaged areas in color, texture and material.</dd></div><div class="term"><dt>Mitigation</dt><dd>Reasonable steps to prevent further damage after a loss, such as tarping or drying.</dd></div><div class="term" id="g-O"><dt>Overhead and profit (O&amp;P)</dt><dd>A general contractor’s charges for coordinating a job, often 10% plus 10% on multi-trade repairs.</dd></div><div class="term" id="g-P"><dt>Proof of claim</dt><dd>The documentation an insurer needs to evaluate a claim. California’s 40-day decision period runs from its receipt.</dd></div><div class="term" id="g-R"><dt>Recoverable depreciation</dt><dd>Depreciation you can collect after completing repairs under a replacement cost policy.</dd></div><div class="term"><dt>Replacement cost value (RCV)</dt><dd>The cost to repair or replace with like kind and quality, without depreciation.</dd></div><div class="term" id="g-S"><dt>Scope of loss</dt><dd>The list of damaged items and the repairs needed to restore them.</dd></div><div class="term"><dt>State of emergency</dt><dd>A declaration by the Governor or President that can trigger added insurance protections in California.</dd></div><div class="term"><dt>Suit limitation</dt><dd>The policy deadline to file a lawsuit against the insurer, subject to California tolling rules.</dd></div><div class="term"><dt>Supplement</dt><dd>A request for additional payment for items missing or under-priced in the original estimate.</dd></div><div class="term" id="g-U"><dt>Umpire</dt><dd>The neutral third panel member who decides items the two appraisers cannot agree on.</dd></div><div class="term" id="g-X"><dt>Xactimate</dt><dd>Widely used estimating software for insurance repair estimates, with regional price lists.</dd></div></dl></article>
